{"kind":"report","text":"Checker review of the inactive J4 setup Source V8 and its proposed qualification, for request 45bb65f4. Verdict: APPROVED, as inactive Source and as the scope of the qualifier to author, with one change for the next version. This review adopts no operator.\n\nThe Source is `/private/tmp/artroom-j4-setup-equivalence-implementation-source-20261031-v8`, with `source-index.json` f5753bda and `PINS.json` 493e8f38. All 58 index rows verify, and the read-only evidence copy matches. It releases no admission, read or setup, and claims no J4 or S1 result. I ran no independent second read.\n\n## What I checked\n\n- `operator.mjs` differs from V7's core by the three-line comment only, and `admission-context.mjs` is byte-identical to V7.\n- Against V14, `safety`, `enrollment`, `rules-exchange`, `predecessor`, `setup-native` and `process_owner` have no changed lines. `setup.mjs` changes one line, for the output name.\n- I read the `recovery.mjs` and `collector.py` deltas. They move fixed values to names and add the context record. `kind` changes to `j4-setup-once-112-v1` and the packet version to 15, so a V14 record can't load as this one.\n- A search of the changed leaves for V14 names finds only the historical predecessor input path, `/private/tmp/artroom-j4-v14-setup-source-20261031-v4/...`, which is correct. That path sits in `/private/tmp`, and `INPUT-PINS.json` says so.\n- The V9 worktree is clean at 5dc9765f today.\n\nI sampled `fixed-value-audit.json` and did not verify all 49 rows. I did not run the assembler or any parity vector myself.\n\n## Questions\n\na. Yes, R18's behavior is acceptable for a first live run, with one change. Retrying a 403 on a lane that was just created is reasonable, because the session rule reaches a lane through its recorded birth in Directory, and that may lag. Twelve failures then halt with originals kept and nothing resent, which is the honest outcome. The change: `pending-no-outcome-observed` hides why the poll failed, and the S1 403s are the open question this run could answer. Record the last read's fixed result in the public result, for example `{ok:false, reason:\"forbidden\"}` or the status, without any response body. It's a small operator change, and it must land before the adopting review, since it changes the operator's digest.\n\nb. Your reading is right, and my wording was incomplete. `git show` shows the immutable act, and `statement.retired` from `gs inspect` shows a later retirement, so the release read needs both. Stop on retirement. `stale: true` is ordinary for this act, because its bases have moved on.\n\nc. Yes, reading at load is acceptable. It refuses closed when the leaf is absent or null. In `recovery.mjs`, a name file could otherwise name its own packet root, but `executing-recovery-path` ties the module to `FIXED.packet`, and the packet binding pins the leaf's digest. `packet_plan.py` compares the digest at line 110. I did not confirm that it ties its own path to `PACKET` in the same way. Add one case for that in the qualifier: a leaf outside the named packet root refuses, in both languages.\n\nd. Keep the dependency for this run. The V13 `loadAdmission` is the reviewed check of the chain back to enrollment, and rewriting it would need its own review for no gain in a scratch run. Stop 10 is the right guard. The daily worktree sweep never removes anything under `.builder-worktrees`, so the V9 and landed worktrees are safe from it, though nobody should commit in either. Don't carry the dependency into a real-room design.\n\ne. Yes, that scope is right. The plan proves named in-process boundaries against a fake transport, and it says what it leaves unproved. The 14 added cases each test a boundary this work added or relies on, and porting 23 existing cases costs little. That's consistent with `docs/testing.md`, since these are boundary witnesses and not a sweep. Two additions: the leaf-location case from point c, and a check that the R18 result field from point a appears in public output with no response body. Keep the three equivalence negatives and the one control as they stand.\n\n## Points for the adopting review\n\n1. That review must see the filled names, the qualifier's actual capture and the final operator. `review-inputs.PENDING.json` is labelled pending, so don't reuse it blindly once the operator changes for point a.\n2. Stop 13 says that nothing yet compares `result.readFloor` with a later consumer of the setup result. Close that or state it plainly by then.\n3. Most setup writes have no proof under da13242c, and R28 to R30 have none under any Worker. The Membership-window limit is now in the operator's comment and in `PLAN.md`, and the J4 report must carry it.\n\n## Architecture\n\nThe flat layout, the split between current and historical source, and a single names file keep the values auditable. The operator holds no name, so its digest stays stable. Approved.\n\n## Security\n\nThe names can't change the operator. A null or malformed name refuses in both languages, and the once kind changed so that an old record can't load. Approved, with the point a change, which adds a fixed word and no authority.\n\n## Simplification\n\nThe packet is large for what it does, and that comes from V14's custody design, which this version doesn't reopen. One names file replaces 19 scattered values. Approved.\n\n## Conclusions\n\n- Architecture: approved.\n- Security: approved, with the point a change.\n- Simplification: approved.\n- Author the qualifier as planned, with the two additions, and bring the next version with the filled names and the R18 field. This is not operator adoption.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:dc257d656bc5cda94ce4c32b4136e2f0a1a946b1 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:01e0dd696d953e8a4209c9e0a7461afc19c30b9a, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:043d587ef18b62fdeee6b57f8968de156720